WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.149
India now has a carbon market.

2
00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:04.340
So it must cap total emissions, right?

3
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.843
It doesn't.

4
00:00:06.193 --> 00:00:07.857
There is no cap on the total.

5
00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:10.120
The Carbon Credit Trading

6
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.933
Scheme, notified in 2023,

7
00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.973
under the Energy Conservation Act,

8
00:00:14.093 --> 00:00:16.163
sets emission intensity targets:

9
00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:18.062
emissions per tonne of product.

10
00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:19.887
Beat your target, and you

11
00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:21.648
earn carbon credit certificates.

12
00:00:21.798 --> 00:00:23.590
Miss it, and you must buy them.

13
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.650
Targets are notified for

14
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.730
sectors like cement, aluminium,

15
00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.139
paper and refineries.

16
00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:31.166
As of November 2026, iron

17
00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:33.193
and steel is still a draft.

18
00:00:33.643 --> 00:00:35.044
So a plant can grow, and

19
00:00:35.044 --> 00:00:36.736
its total emissions can rise,

20
00:00:36.856 --> 00:00:38.584
while it still meets its target.

21
00:00:39.084 --> 00:00:40.517
The scheme limits how dirty

22
00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:41.260
each tonne is,

23
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.811
not how much is made.

24
00:00:43.311 --> 00:00:44.185
Your turn.

25
00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:45.707
What kind of target does

26
00:00:45.707 --> 00:00:47.194
India's carbon market set?
